WEBVTT
TITLE Mean value theorems: Rolle, Lagrange and Cauchy, and what each one licenses — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
The chord joining the two ends of an interval has a computable slope.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
On x squared over 0 to 2 the chord slope is 2.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
The theorem promises a point c where the derivative equals 2, and here c = 1.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
Parallel, not equal: the tangent at c has the chord’s slope, not its position.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
Rolle’s theorem is the mean value theorem with a flat chord.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
x squared minus 4x plus 3 on 1 to 3 has level ends, and the derivative vanishes at 2.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
The general theorem follows from Rolle by subtracting the chord.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
On the same example the auxiliary function is x squared minus 2x, flat-ended on 0 to 2.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Continuity on the closed interval, differentiability on the open one, and that is all.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
Absolute x on minus 1 to 1 has level ends, and its derivative is never 0.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
Break continuity at an endpoint and the conclusion can fail too.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
Differentiability at the endpoints is not required: root x on 0 to 1 gives c = 0.25.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
The theorem promises at least one point. It never promises only one.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
x cubed minus x on minus 1 to 1 has level ends and two admissible points.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
On the identity over 0 to 1, every interior point is admissible.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
So a question asking for the point is ill-posed unless uniqueness is forced.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
Rewrite the conclusion: the change equals a derivative times the width.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
Sine: the difference of two sines is at most the gap between the arguments.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
Exponential: e to the x exceeds 1 plus x for every positive x.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
Root 101 is trapped between 10.049751 and 10.05 by one secant slope.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
A positive derivative on an interval forces the function to increase.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
A derivative that is zero everywhere on an interval forces a constant.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
The converse fails: x cubed is strictly increasing and its derivative is 0 at the origin.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
On a domain in two pieces, a zero derivative everywhere still does not give a constant.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
Cauchy’s version compares two functions at one and the same point.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
x squared against x cubed on 1 to 2 gives a change ratio of 3 over 7, and c = 14 over 9.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
Applied separately, the two theorems give 1.5 and 1.527525 — different points.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
That is why l’Hôpital’s rule needs the two-function version.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
Between two zeros of a differentiable function there is a zero of its derivative.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
x cubed minus 3x plus 1 has three zeros: minus 1.879385, 0.347296 and 1.532089.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
The derivative 3 x squared minus 3 vanishes at minus 1 and at 1, one in each gap.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
Read backwards: a degree n polynomial has at most n real zeros.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
Four results look like this one and answer different questions.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
On x squared over 0 to 2 the differential point is 1, the integral point is 1.154701.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
The intermediate value theorem places values, not slopes.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
Derivatives keep the intermediate value property even where they are discontinuous.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
First: are the three hypotheses actually met?

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Second: is a point wanted, a bound, or a count?

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Third: solve, then count the solutions inside the interval.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
Finally: bound the answer before choosing an option.

